course: dictionary
lesson: 2639
title: Spotting details
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A detail is a small fact or part of something. Details are the small things that make a picture clear. «Small details like a car's colour, a number plate or what someone wore help police a lot.» The colour, the number plate and the clothes are small facts, and together they can help find a car or a person.
explain: Details can also mean information about you, such as your name, address and phone number. These are called contact details or personal details. «Phishing means tricking someone into giving private details like a login or card number.» Never give your personal details to a stranger, even if they seem friendly.
explain: To go into detail is to describe something fully, with all its small parts. «A large-scale map, such as 1:25,000, shows a small area in great detail, while a small-scale map, such as 1:10,000,000, shows a huge area with little detail.» So the more detail a map shows, the more of its small parts you can see.
fact: A detail is a small fact or part of something.
fact: Details are information such as a name, address or phone number.
fact: «In detail» means fully, including all the small parts.
fact: To detail something means to describe it fully.
fact: «Clothes, colours and special marks are easy details to notice about a person.»
exercise: «Small details like a car's colour, a number plate or what someone wore help police a lot.» Which of these is a small detail that helps police? | *The car's number plate | The day the car was bought | The name of the country | The weather in the city
exercise: Which of these are personal details? | The colour of the sky | *Your name and phone number | The name of your favourite film | The number of stairs in your school
exercise: «The map showed a small area in great detail.» What does «in great detail» mean? | Only the big things, and nothing else | Without any colour | Very quickly, in a hurry | *Fully, with all the small parts
test: «To detail something is to ...» | lose it | hide it under a rug | throw it away | *describe it fully
test: Which sentence adds detail to a description? | The boy was there | Someone came in | It was nice | *The tall boy wore a red coat with gold buttons
exam: Lily writes a story about a lost dog. Which is the best way to add detail? | Leave the dog out of the story | Say the dog was good | Write the word nice many times | *Describe the dog's brown ears and muddy paws
